    "# Question 1 — Gradient Descent Concepts\n",
    "\n",
    "The assignment asks for these answers **in your own words**, so the explanations below are intentionally direct and natural.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1(a) What is gradient descent? What functions can it be used on? What does it do?\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent is an iterative method for finding a **minimum** of a function.\n",
    "\n",
    "At the current point, it calculates the derivative or gradient and moves in the opposite direction because the gradient points toward the direction of fastest increase.\n",
    "\n",
    "For one variable:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "x_{k+1}=x_k-\\eta f'(x_k)\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For several variables:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\mathbf{x}_{k+1}\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\mathbf{x}_k-\\eta\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_k)\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "It is mainly used on functions for which derivatives or gradients can be calculated.\n",
    "\n",
    "Its goal is to move from a starting point toward a point where the objective function is smaller, ideally reaching a local or global minimum.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Simple definition\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\text{Gradient descent repeatedly moves downhill to reduce a function.}\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1(b) Will gradient descent always work?\n",
    "\n",
    "No.\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent can fail or behave poorly when:\n",
    "\n",
    "- the learning rate is too large, causing the iterates to overshoot or diverge;\n",
    "- the learning rate is too small, making convergence very slow;\n",
    "- the function has several local minima;\n",
    "- the starting point is poor;\n",
    "- the gradient is zero at a point that is not the desired minimum;\n",
    "- the function is not sufficiently differentiable.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a well-behaved convex function and a suitable learning rate, gradient descent has much stronger convergence behavior.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1(c) What is a hyperparameter?\n",
    "\n",
    "A hyperparameter is a setting chosen **before or outside the learning process** that controls how the algorithm behaves.\n",
    "\n",
    "For gradient descent, the learning rate\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\eta\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "is a hyperparameter.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is not normally learned by the gradient-descent update itself; instead, we choose or tune it.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\text{Hyperparameter = a user-chosen setting controlling the learning algorithm.}\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
