    "## 3(b) $$x_0=-1,\\;\\eta=0.01$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Notice that\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f'(-1)=0.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "So gradient descent does not move:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1=x_0=-1,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and every later iterate is also $$-1$$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{x_{100}=-1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "y_{100}=f(-1)=\\frac{11}{3}\\approx3.6666666667.\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is an important lesson: if gradient descent starts exactly at a critical point, the gradient is zero and the algorithm can remain there."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c67bb045",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3(c) $$x_0=1,\\;\\eta=0.1$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here the learning rate is too large for this starting point.\n",
    "\n",
    "The iterations overshoot and the magnitude of $$x_k$$ grows extremely rapidly.\n",
    "\n",
    "With ordinary Python floating-point arithmetic, the computation eventually produces an `OverflowError` because values become too large to represent.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore there is **no finite numerical value for $$x_{100}$$ or $$y_{100}$$** from this run.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\text{The gradient-descent iteration diverges / overflows.}\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The reason is the combination of the starting point and the large learning rate:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\eta=0.1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Large updates cause repeated overshooting, and because this is a quartic function, its derivative grows very rapidly when $$|x|$$ becomes large."
   ]
  },
