    "# Question 5 — Lagrange Multipliers\n",
    "\n",
    "Minimize\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "subject to\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x^2+y^2=1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Define the constraint\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "g(x,y)=x^2+y^2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Lagrange multipliers require\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\nabla f\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\lambda\\nabla g.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\nabla f=\n",
    "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
    "2x\\\\\n",
    "4y\n",
    "\\end{pmatrix},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\nabla g=\n",
    "\\begin{pmatrix}\n",
    "2x\\\\\n",
    "2y\n",
    "\\end{pmatrix}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "So\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "2x=2\\lambda x,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "4y=2\\lambda y,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "together with\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x^2+y^2=1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The candidate points are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "(\\pm1,0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "(0,\\pm1).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Evaluate the objective:\n",
    "\n",
    "At $$(\\pm1,0)$$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f=1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At $$(0,\\pm1)$$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f=2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore the absolute minimum is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "at\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{(1,0)\\text{ and }(-1,0)}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why ordinary gradient descent is difficult on a constraint curve\n",
    "\n",
    "A normal gradient-descent step follows\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "-\\nabla f.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "But that direction does not necessarily stay on the circle\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x^2+y^2=1.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "So a standard update can leave the feasible set immediately.\n",
    "\n",
    "Constrained problems therefore need methods such as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Lagrange multipliers;\n",
    "- projected gradient descent;\n",
    "- other constrained optimization algorithms."
   ]
  },
