    "# THEORY CHEAT SHEET — Week 05\n",
    "\n",
    "This section is designed for quick understanding and revision."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6a4063fa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Gradient Descent\n",
    "\n",
    "Core update:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\mathbf{x}_{k+1}\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\mathbf{x}_k-\\eta\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_k)\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "- gradient = direction of fastest increase;\n",
    "- negative gradient = direction of fastest decrease;\n",
    "- $$\\eta$$ = learning rate.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Small $$\\eta$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Usually safer but slower.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Large $$\\eta$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Can overshoot, oscillate, or diverge.\n",
    "\n",
    "### AI connection\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent is one of the main optimization ideas used to train machine-learning and neural-network models."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "66bec2a6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Second Derivative Test — One Variable\n",
    "\n",
    "At a critical point $$x^*$$ where\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f'(x^*)=0:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "If\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f''(x^*)>0,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "then local minimum.\n",
    "\n",
    "If\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f''(x^*)<0,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "then local maximum.\n",
    "\n",
    "If\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f''(x^*)=0,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the test is inconclusive."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "27dff928",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Second Derivative Test — Two Variables\n",
    "\n",
    "At a critical point define\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-(f_{xy})^2.\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "- $$D>0$$ and $$f_{xx}>0$$: local minimum;\n",
    "- $$D>0$$ and $$f_{xx}<0$$: local maximum;\n",
    "- $$D<0$$: saddle point;\n",
    "- $$D=0$$: inconclusive."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "80d5fda2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Hyperparameter\n",
    "\n",
    "A hyperparameter is chosen outside the model-learning update.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "- learning rate;\n",
    "- number of iterations;\n",
    "- batch size;\n",
    "- regularization strength.\n",
    "\n",
    "For gradient descent:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\\eta=\\text{learning-rate hyperparameter}}\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b24ebf0e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Lagrange Multipliers\n",
    "\n",
    "To optimize\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(x,y)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "subject to\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "g(x,y)=c,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "solve\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\nabla f=\\lambda\\nabla g\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "together with\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "g(x,y)=c.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Geometrically, at a constrained optimum the objective and constraint gradients are parallel."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0c2f0c3f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Linear Programming\n",
    "\n",
    "A linear program has:\n",
    "\n",
    "- a linear objective;\n",
    "- linear constraints;\n",
    "- a feasible region.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a bounded feasible polygon, a linear objective achieves its optimum at a vertex.\n",
    "\n",
    "So the graphical method is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\text{find feasible vertices}\n",
    "\\rightarrow\n",
    "\\text{evaluate objective}\n",
    "\\rightarrow\n",
    "\\text{choose best}\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6e6f9e97",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Objective Level Lines\n",
    "\n",
    "For\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f=3x+y,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "a fixed objective value $$c$$ gives\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "3x+y=c.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "All these lines have the same slope:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "-3.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Increasing $$c$$ shifts the line parallel to itself.\n",
    "\n",
    "This explains graphically why an LP optimum occurs when the last possible objective line touches the feasible set."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e49cfe43",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Primal–Dual Pattern\n",
    "\n",
    "For the primal\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\max c^Tx\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "subject to\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Ax\\le b,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "x\\ge0,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "the dual is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\min b^Ty\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "subject to\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "A^Ty\\ge c,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "y\\ge0.\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At an optimum under standard conditions,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "\\text{primal optimal value}\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\text{dual optimal value}.\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5beb8987",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Probability Rules\n",
    "\n",
    "### Complement\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "P(A^c)=1-P(A)\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Union\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "P(A\\cup B)\n",
    "=\n",
    "P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Conditional probability\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "P(A|B)\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Independence\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Mutually exclusive\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "P(A\\cap B)=0\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Do not confuse independence with mutual exclusivity."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e280feb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 10. Quick Recognition Guide\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **learning rate**, think gradient descent.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **critical point classification**, think second derivative test.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **constraint equality** with nonlinear objective, think Lagrange multipliers.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **maximize/minimize a linear function with linear inequalities**, think linear programming.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **primal and dual**, transpose the constraint structure and reverse max/min appropriately.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **given that**, think conditional probability.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **independent**, multiply probabilities for intersections.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you see **mutually exclusive**, the intersection probability is zero."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c553b063",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 11. Common Mistakes\n",
    "\n",
    "- Using the gradient instead of the **negative** gradient for minimization.\n",
    "- Assuming gradient descent always reaches the global minimum.\n",
    "- Choosing a learning rate that is too large.\n",
    "- Forgetting to test every critical point.\n",
    "- Forgetting the original constraint in a Lagrange-multiplier problem.\n",
    "- Checking only one LP vertex.\n",
    "- Confusing a dual inequality direction.\n",
    "- Confusing independent events with mutually exclusive events.\n",
    "- Forgetting the intersection term in the union formula.\n",
    "- Using unconditional probability when the problem says “given that.”"
   ]
  },
